{"kind":"report","text":"Approved at exact head 63ab4c4374a546f2409dd4293e42503a6bd54d43 (tree e2ae42c7a6129ab13de0325b2413ae571e527e3c), E3 checked client views on published SG-1 c9038871. The verdict binds the E3 implementation promise 8fb82297 (request 17e075b3) and signs all 28 path artifacts, with scope-client.ts (ac86d183) first. It authorizes no deploy. The planner decides the landing and the deploy.\n\n## Evidence\n- **Artifacts.** The 28 filed artifacts equal the 28 changed paths (`git diff --no-renames --name-only c9038871 63ab4c43`): none missing, none extra, none twice.\n- **Gate.** The full gate at 65e9d5ea failed (`gate.txt` SHA-256 `edbabfa5…`, exit 1, 226.37 s). Static checks, types, definition pins, pins and Kernel passed. Node passed 245 of 246 cases, and Counting, Plain and Native were unreached. The failing case is \"interrupting the wrapper also stops the detached test worker's owned listener\" in `apps/counter/scripts/node-witness.test.mjs`. The follow-up at 96401fe9 changes only that file (`git diff --name-only 65e9d5ea 96401fe9`). It ran 20 serial whole-file runs and one separate owner run, then Counting 60 (exit 0), Plain 5,140 with one skipped (exit 0, 539.29 s) and Native 1,240 with two skipped (exit 0, 618.60 s, 1,158.61 s in all). Native crossings: zero lost, five stale, zero unowned. The head differs from 96401fe9 only in the evidence note (`git diff --name-only 96401fe9 63ab4c43`). The failed gate stays a failure in the record, and the note does not relabel it. I assess the follow-up under docs/evidence-filing.md below.\n- **Pins.** The pins at published c9038871 and at 65e9d5ea both passed, and their logs are retained. Two open presentation identifiers changed, Lending (`c3ba4ba6…` to `c0d9b2fb…`) and Counter (`8c4a18a5…` to `6abe81e2…`), and each old value is at the base. No signed release packet path is in the diff. The Page module `apps/room/page/assets.ts` moves from `7bb232eb…` (base) to `d8371485…` (head), which I recomputed with `shasum -a 256`. Its Page and OAuth JavaScript digests move from base values `d8406c0d…` and `e89becaf…`, and the new values sit in the file.\n- **Hashes.** The note carries 45 distinct SHA-256 values. 33 match files in the retained evidence directories, one matches `e3-generated-cuts.test.ts`, and the other 11 are the generated-module and presentation values above.\n- **Checks.** The merge into origin/main (88072972) is clean. `git diff --check` is clean. `check-commit-messages` flags none of 5 commits. `check-notes` reports 0 warnings. The added lines carry no other project's name and no `/Users/` path outside the retained evidence directory references.\n- **Scale tier.** The continuation record shows the selector omitted scale (`outside-scale-paths`). The selector is repository code, and the GS-2 audit of its breadth is the open item that covers it.\n- **Landing.** The base moved from c9038871 to 88072972, a one-note change that this head does not touch, so the gate stands (ruling 6c0b1332). The landing check follows the merge.\n\n## Wake rate\nNo alarm, retry or reschedule is added, and no Durable Object alarm owner is added. The client `#armView` keeps one client-side timer per publication (the earlier timer is cancelled) at the nearest of the session's `ends` and a timed rule's deadline. The wake floor is unchanged, and requests per room per hour are unchanged. Nothing in `apps`, `examples`, or the scope, CLI and MCP sources passes `viewAuthority` or `ticks`, so no deployed screen makes an extra request. The first adopter's `viewAuthority.read` runs once for each publication, and when the replica is mid-read the loop joins that read and flags one more pass (`replica.read()` sets `#again`). An adapter must hold and reuse its session, because a request per head change multiplies by viewers. Review that adapter for its requests per room per hour.\n\n## Architecture\n- A view captures one cut: an immutable clone of the published state, the held entries, the head and one clock reading. `explore` and `offers` judge on that cut and make no read or signing call. The head, the held journal and the published head must name one cut, or the cut is null.\n- `#sync` clones nothing after its last await. After the adapter read, it checks `authority.current`, the exploration generation and `#again`, and then computes and publishes synchronously. A change during an await invalidates the whole candidate. `close` discards an in-flight candidate, and a closed or halted view answers every complete exploration `unavailable` with `source`.\n- The projection keeps the numeric C4 `row` and `rows` unchanged. `projectedRows` adds checked `ActRow` values and the symbolic `ref`. A pending act whose source cannot be proven, or whose projection fails to admit, gets `unavailable: \"source\"` and no `ref`. A legacy custody row gets `unavailable: \"legacy-custody\"`, `ref: null` and `recorded: null`.\n\n## Security\n- **No authority from a stranger.** `checkedViewSession` trusts a `Session` only if `requestSession` recorded it at its answer boundary, after `sessionStanding` passed, and only if its reader, claims and standing still equal the clones taken then. A `Session` an adapter builds by hand has no record. `e3-view.test.ts` builds a session with a forged role and mutates a standing in place, and both lose authority.\n- **Binding.** `viewJudge` also requires the session to name the signer's key, the member and the membership that the genesis entry of the cut derives. `scopeUsesSession` ties it to the scope. The acquisition start must exist and not follow the reading, and the session must not have ended. Any failure yields no authority, and a complete exploration answers `authority-unavailable`.\n- **Fail closed.** A definition with capabilities, operations or records yields `source`. A missing or unsafe clock gives a null reading and an `unavailable` exploration with a null clock. `unavailable` is the default on every error path.\n- **Stale cut.** A held view keeps judging its own cut after the live head moves, and it never mixes a later journal (`e3-view-cut.test.ts`, the case-7 witness).\n- **Legacy.** A pending request without a recorded authorization has no source reference. Any explore that names it, or any view that holds one, answers `legacy-custody` or `source`. That fails closed.\n- **Seals.** `session.ts` and the new `checked-view-session.ts` have updated seals in `security-entry-points.test.mjs`. The gate's Node stage passed that test on the first head, and the later head changes only the Counter test and the note.\n- **Release.** No signed release, trust row or token path changes. The gateway is unchanged.\n\n## Simplification\n- `legacyIds` and `legacyValue` in `explore` only choose the lack reason when `checked` is absent. Whenever a legacy pending row exists, `failed` is already true and returns `legacy-custody`. A later pass can drop that block.\n- The note and README state the behavior plainly. Nothing else to remove.\n\n## The Counter test follow-up\nThe Counter repair stays inside the documented route: one test file changes, no other file imports it (`scripts/test.mjs` only lists its path), the whole file reran, and the stages the gate never reached ran. The original case, its 1,000 ms bound and its assertion that the worker releases the listener remain. The repair makes two changes. The ready line must now end with a newline, so a split chunk cannot yield a truncated port. The listener probe now polls for refusal up to the original 1,000 ms instead of testing once. That is a bounded allowance, not an immediate check, so it is somewhat weaker. The measured maximum was 0.8 ms in 20 runs, which makes it unobtrusive in practice. The retained `node.jsonl` has no error text for the first failure, so the note cannot say which of the two causes fired. The repair covers both.\n\n## Non-blocking\n1. **Stale seam comment.** `Seam` in `replica.ts` is still labelled \"TEST SEAM\" and `scope.ts` does not export it. `scope-client.ts` and `view-cut.ts` now read it in production code, and E3 adds `generation()` and `reading()`. Update the comment to say production uses it, or rename the seam.\n2. **One unprojectable pending row stops every exploration in its view.** That includes offers. The behavior is conservative and the note states it. The first adopter (PB-1) should expect it and show the `unavailable` reasons.\n3. **Failure text.** Keep the first node-witness failure's error text in future gate evidence, so a repair names its cause.\n\n## Deployment\nThe Page asset changes, so the room Worker serves a new bundle. The two open presentation pins change with it, as SG-1 did. The pins stage lists any other Worker that needs a deploy. Read the DO rate after the deploy.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:ac86d183d797a0248b4f8d9b09963e3d5652b09f","binding":"assigned","head":"63ab4c4374a546f2409dd4293e42503a6bd54d43","head_news_acknowledged":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:230f35f46c6dc1a74309adb211e2b282fe3bfa85\"]","implementations":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:8fb822970e7b7281e76c698e4481d78c1eefcdf2\"]","review_frontier":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:d8e492651f030bc9cbf506f2a87eb0141f71409f","review_path":"reviewguard@1","verdict":"approved"}}